      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To find the distance between the vertices of the hyperbola given by the equation\n\n\\[\n\\frac{y^2}{27} - \\frac{x^2}{11} = 1,\n\\]\n\nwe need to identify the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) from the standard form of a hyperbola that is horizontally expanding. The standard form for a horizontally expanding hyperbola is\n\n\\[\n\\frac{y^2}{a^2} - \\frac{x^2}{b^2} = 1.\n\\]\n\nBy comparing this with the given equation \\(\\frac{y^2}{27} - \\frac{x^2}{11} = 1\\), we can see that \\(a^2 = 27\\) and \\(b^2 = 11\\). Therefore, \\(a = \\sqrt{27} = 3\\sqrt{3}\\) and \\(b = \\sqrt{11}\\).\n\nFor a hyperbola of the form \\(\\frac{y^2}{a^2} - \\frac{x^2}{b^2} = 1\\), the distance between the vertices is \\(2a\\). Thus, the distance between the vertices is\n\n\\[\n2a = 2 \\times 3\\sqrt{3} = 6\\sqrt{3}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the distance between the vertices of the hyperbola is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6\\sqrt{3}}.\n\\]",
